# T Cloud Public Landing Zone: **${platform_identifier}**

%{ if playground_mode ~}
> **Playground mode.** The platform identifier carries a random suffix so this deployment does not
> occupy a name for good. Do not publish this platform or the building block definition it
> registered to other workspaces. Redeploy with `playground_mode` set to false for a platform that
> is actually used.

%{ endif ~}
## Details

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Domain** | `${domain_name}` |
| **Region** | `${region}` |
| **Landing Zone** | `${landingzone_name}` |
| **Backplane IAM user** | `${backplane_user_name}` |
| **Management project** | `${management_project}` (T Cloud Public `${region}_${management_project}`) |

%{ if !federation_enabled ~}
> [!WARNING]
> ## No identity provider connected: users are managed in T Cloud Public

Application teams can create projects, and every project gets its `admin`, `user` and `reader`
groups, but nobody is put into them. meshStack's project members do not reach T Cloud Public until
an identity provider is connected.

**Until then**, add users to the project groups by hand in the
[T Cloud Public IAM console](https://console.otc.t-systems.com/iam/). The groups are named
`${region}_<project>_<role>`.

**To connect your identity provider:**

1. Register T Cloud Public as an application in your identity provider. These guides cover it:

   | Identity provider | Protocol | Guide |
   |---|---|---|
   | Microsoft Entra ID | OIDC | [Entra ID as identity provider](https://arch.otc-service.com/docs/blueprints/by-use-case/security/microsoft/azure-entraid-as-identity-provider-iam) |
   | Keycloak | OIDC | [Keycloak identity federation](https://arch.otc-service.com/docs/blueprints/by-use-case/security/keycloak/identity-federation-github) |
   | Zitadel | SAML | [Zitadel as identity provider](https://arch.otc-service.com/docs/blueprints/by-use-case/security/zitadel/zitadel-as-identity-provider-iam) |
   | AD FS, Authentik | SAML | [Security blueprints](https://arch.otc-service.com/docs/blueprints/by-use-case/security/) |

   For OIDC, the redirect URIs are `https://auth.otc.t-systems.com/authui/oidc/redirect` and
   `https://auth.otc.t-systems.com/authui/oidc/post`. Only the application registration applies;
   skip the guides' mapping rules, this landing zone writes its own.
2. Make sure the identity provider sends each user's email address, and that it is exactly the one
   meshStack knows for them. Users are matched to projects by email.
3. **Update this building block** and fill in the **Identity Provider** input: the issuer URL,
   client ID and signing keys for OIDC, or the metadata XML for SAML.

The next run federates the identity provider and deploys the function that maps meshStack's
project members into the project groups. Nothing already provisioned is touched.
%{ else ~}
> [!TIP]
> ## Sign-in is federated through `${identity_provider_name}` (${identity_provider_protocol})

meshStack's project members sign in at <${login_link}> with their company account, and receive
their project roles from their next sign-in. They are matched by the `${email_attribute}` claim or
attribute, which has to carry the email address meshStack knows for them.

The mapping is kept in step by @buildingblock[${federation_mapping_bb_uuid}] in the management
project.
%{ endif ~}

## What application teams get

Application teams create meshStack projects in the `${landingzone_name}` landing zone. Each one
becomes a T Cloud Public project `${region}_<project>`, with one IAM group per meshStack role.
